diff --git a/Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/README.md b/Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..907b2de7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,637 @@
+# CinematicCamera
+
+A physically based camera component driven by real lens parameters (Sandbox category:
+**Cameras / Cinematic Camera**):
+
+- **FOV** from focal length + sensor size, written into the sibling `CCameraComponent` the
+  component creates automatically (so the entity shows up in Sandbox's Camera Entity menu and
+  the editor viewport previews it with the correct frustum).
+- **DOF** from aperture + focus distance through the thin-lens equations, written to the
+  `Dof_User_*` post effect params.
+- **Exposure** written to `E3DPARAM_HDR_EYEADAPTATION_PARAMS` from the plugin's
+  `UpdateBeforeFinalizeCamera()` hook (after the Time of Day update, so TOD cannot overwrite
+  it in the same frame).
+- Also drives **Film Grain**, **Motion Blur**, **White Balance**, **lens character**
+  (vignette / distortion), **Diffraction Streaks** (sunstars), **Halation** (the red-orange
+  film halo) and the hybrid **sprite bokeh** highlight splats.
+
+Lens values can be pushed live from a phone fader panel by
+[CryPhoneTracker](../CryPhoneTracker/README.md); the interface is
+`Interface/ICinematicCameraOptics.h` (`CinematicCamera.dll` exports
+`CryGetCinematicCameraOptics(IEntity*)`).
+
+Schematyc functions: `Activate`, `Deactivate`, `IsActive`.
+
+---
+
+## Camera slots and switching
+
+Every cinematic camera can carry a **Camera Slot** number (1-9, `0` = no slot, the default).
+The console command
+
+```
+ccam_slot 3
+```
+
+activates the cinematic camera whose Camera Slot is 3, and logs the entity it switched to
+(or a message when no camera carries that slot). Bind the slots to the number keys to cut
+between cameras while the game runs:
+
+```
+bind 1 ccam_slot 1
+bind 2 ccam_slot 2
+bind 3 ccam_slot 3
+```
+
+A Phone Tracker with **Follow Active Camera** enabled moves along with each cut, re-anchoring
+on the new camera. See [docs/ConsoleReference.md](docs/ConsoleReference.md) for the command
+reference.
+
+The same switch is available to other plugins as the exported C entry points
+`CryActivateCinematicCameraSlot(int slot)` and `CryGetActiveCinematicCameraEntity()`.
+
+## Single authority: who owns the render state
+
+The **camera manager decides** which camera is active; the component only caches its answer.
+Once per frame the plugin hook calls the resolver, which walks the registry of live cinematic
+cameras, finds the one the manager considers active, and — when the answer changed — makes
+the dethroned component restore every render state it had taken over. Only the
+resolver-confirmed active component may write DOF, grain, motion blur, white balance,
+streaks, sprite bokeh, vignette/distortion or exposure; components in a Preview simulation
+(asset browser preview windows) never write them at all.
+
+`Activate()` activates the sibling `CCameraComponent`, which IS the ownership transfer.
+
+`Deactivate()` does **not** null the manager's active camera — the engine always needs some
+active view. Its meaning is: strip this component's cinematic effects (full restore sweep)
+and mark it suspended, so the entity stays a plain view until another camera activates.
+`IsActive()` reports false while suspended, and `Activate()` clears the suspension.
+
+**Activate On Game Start** must stay opt-in: every component receives START_GAME, so with
+several cinematic cameras in a level the last one to get the event would silently steal the
+view from an explicit Schematyc `Activate` call.
+
+## Edit mode policy
+
+Effects split by whether they depend on the view:
+
+| Effect | Editor (edit mode) | Game / simulation mode |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Depth of field, sprite bokeh, motion blur, film grain, white balance, diffraction streaks | not applied | applied |
+| Vignette, distortion (lens character) | live | applied |
+| Exposure | live | applied |
+
+In edit mode the viewport renders through the editor fly camera, so pushing this camera's
+focus and blur onto it would produce meaningless imagery. View-independent character stays
+live so it can be authored with immediate feedback.
+
+Entering game/simulation mode drops every saved baseline without writing anything (the
+editor's `ResetPostEffects()` has already wiped the renderer, so the captured values are
+stale); leaving it runs the full restore sweep. Note the stock editor behaviour: property
+edits made inside game mode are reverted when leaving it.
+
+The preview `CCameraComponent` this component creates is flagged `NoSave` — it is ours, for
+the editor's benefit, and must never be serialized. Levels saved before that flag existed
+accumulated one extra camera component per save/load cycle; a repair pass removes those
+duplicates on Initialize and again on LEVEL_LOADED. A camera component the user added
+deliberately keeps its persistence and is never removed.
+
+---
+
+## Property overview (by inspector group)
+
+The "Range" column is the hard limit (Schematyc Range); where the slider has a narrower soft
+range it is noted in brackets.
+
+### Camera Body
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Camera Slot | 0-9 | 0 | The number this camera answers to. `ccam_slot N` in the console (bind it to a key) makes it the active camera, and a Phone Tracker set to follow the active camera comes along with it. 0 = no slot. |
+| Activate On Game Start | bool | false | Automatically become the active camera when gameplay starts. Leave off when Schematyc decides which camera is active. |
+| Near Plane (m) | 0-32768 | 0.25 | Near clip plane. |
+| Far Plane (m) | 0-32768 | 1024 | Far clip plane. |
+| Sensor Preset | enum | Full Frame 35mm | Reference sensor size used for FOV and circle of confusion: Full Frame 36x24mm, APS-C 23.6x15.7mm, Micro Four Thirds 17.3x13mm, Custom. |
+| Custom Sensor Width (mm) | 1-200 | 36 | Only used when the preset is Custom; height is width x 2/3. |
+| ISO | 25-25600 (slider 50-6400) | 400 | Sensitivity. Drives Manual/AutoBiased exposure and the film grain curve. |
+| Shutter (1/x s) | 1-8000 (slider 1-250) | 50 | Shutter denominator. 50 is roughly a 180 degree shutter at 25 fps. Drives exposure, the motion blur shutter and the grain light term. |
+
+### Lens
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Focal Length (mm) | 1-2400 | 50 | Shorter = wider angle. Drives the vertical field of view. |
+| Focal Step Mode | enum | Prime Lens Set | Quantise the focal length so zooming has detents instead of sliding freely. See *Focal stepping and servo*. |
+| Steps Per Doubling | 1-24 (slider 2-12) | 6 | Detents per doubling of focal length — only used by Geometric. |
+| Focal Step Time | 0-2 s (slider 0-1) | 0.25 | Seconds the zoom motor takes to settle on a new detent. 0 snaps instantly. |
+| Aperture (f-number) | 1-64 | 5.6 | Lower f-number = shallower depth of field. Clamped to f/0.7 in the maths. |
+| Aperture Blades | 3-16 (slider 5-12) | 9 | Iris blade count. Shapes the bokeh polygon and the number of sunstar rays. |
+| Blade Curvature | 0-1 | 0.4 | How curved the iris blades are, i.e. whether the aperture can actually close to a circle. 0 = perfectly straight blades: the full polygon shows at every f-stop, so a 3-blade iris stays a rounded triangle even wide open. 1 = fully curved blades that retract into a true circle wide open. Real lenses sit in between, and low blade counts show it most. |
+| Transmission | 0-1 (clamped 0.25-1 in use) | 1.0 | Fraction of light the glass passes; defines the T-stop. 1 = ideal glass, modern prime ~0.9, zoom ~0.8, vintage ~0.75. See *T-stop*. |
+| ND Filter (stops) | 0-10 (slider 0-6) | 0 | Neutral density in front of the lens: darkens by whole stops without touching depth of field or motion blur. |
+| Focus Distance (m) | 0-10000 | 5 | Distance to the sharp focus plane. |
+| Vignette Amount | 0-2 | 0 | Optical vignetting strength. 0 = none, 1 = the engine's stock vignette map, 2 = deepened. |
+| Vignette Aperture Link | 0-1 | 0 | How much the vignette follows the iris (mechanical vignetting): 0 = fixed amount, 1 = strongest wide open and gone by f/5.6. |
+| Distortion | -1 to 1 | 0 | Radial lens distortion: positive = barrel, negative = pincushion / wide-angle correction. A fixed per-lens character, deliberately not tied to focal length. |
+| Distortion Focal Link | -1 to 1 | 0 | Optional zoom characteristic: positive values add barrel at short focals and pincushion at long ones (pivot 35mm). 0 = prime-like fixed distortion. |
+| Focus Breathing | 0-2 (slider 0-1) | 1.0 | How much the field of view tightens when focusing close. 0 = perfectly corrected cine lens, 1 = typical stills lens, 2 = vintage glass. |
+| Use Sprite Bokeh | bool | false | Add scattered bokeh sprites for out-of-focus HIGHLIGHTS on top of the normal depth of field blur, the way film builds them up: bright points grow into readable discs with a bright rim instead of being averaged flat. The base blur is unchanged, so scenes without bright highlights look the same either way. Required for Bokeh Shape Texture to have any effect. |
+| Bokeh Shape Texture | texture | (empty) | Silhouette (red channel) of a physical mask mounted in front of the lens. Empty = procedural disc. Only used when Use Sprite Bokeh is on. See *Shape mask and Mask Aperture*. |
+| Mask Aperture | 1-22 (slider 1-8) | 2.0 | Equivalent f-number of the shape mask's bounding circle. The pupil is iris ∩ mask at every f-number: iris wider than this and the mask bounds the pupil (its shape rules, the polygon only trims what pokes past it); iris stopped down past it and the polygon bounds the pupil, clipping the silhouette corner by corner. Depth of field and bokeh SIZE follow whichever hole is the smaller one. Only used when a Bokeh Shape Texture is set. |
+| Mask Rotation (deg) | 0-360 | 0 | Rotation of the shape mask around the optical axis, independent of Blade Rotation (the mask is its own element): a note cut-out turns while the blade polygon and the sunstars stay put. Applies to the splatted silhouette and the soft gather halo alike. |
+| Mask Open Area | 0-1 | 1.0 | Transmittance of the mask's OPEN parts (a tinted gel in the cut-out; 1 = clear). The blocked part is measured from the texture itself: light reaching the sensor = area(iris ∩ mask) / area(iris) × this value, through the same T-stop term as glass transmission, so exposure and film grain react like a neutral density loss - continuously, at every f-number. |
+
+### Anamorphic
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Squeeze Factor | 1-2 | 1.0 | 1 = spherical. 1.33 / 1.5 / 1.8 / 2 = anamorphic: the horizontal capture widens by this factor for the same focal length, out-of-focus discs become vertical ovals of this ratio, and the frame is masked to the desqueezed format whenever that format is wider than the viewport. See *Anamorphic*. |
+| Horizontal Flare | 0-3 (slider 0-2) | 0.0 | Strength of the anamorphic streak: the long horizontal line thrown across the frame by bright sources off the cylindrical front element. 0 = off. Strongest wide open, gone by f/11. Independent of the diffraction streaks. |
+| Flare Length | 0-1 | 0.7 | How far the line reaches across the frame. |
+| Flare Threshold | 0-50 (slider 0-20) | 3.0 | Scene HDR value a source must exceed to throw a flare. |
+| Flare Tint | colour | (0.35, 0.62, 1.0) | Colour of the flare. Classic anamorphic coatings give the cyan-blue line; a neutral tint gives white. |
+| Show Squeezed Frame | bool | false | Present the raw anamorphic negative instead of the projected frame: the letterbox band is stretched vertically to fill the viewport, so the whole picture carries the squeeze, not just the bokeh. Same frustum either way. |
+
+### Exposure
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Enable Exposure Control | bool | false | Override the HDR eye-adaptation EV compensation. |
+| Exposure Mode | enum | Auto + Bias (game) | AutoCompensation / Manual / AutoBiased, see below. |
+| Exposure Response | 0-1 | 0.6 | Engine stops per photographic stop, compressed around Neutral EV. 1 = strict physics (each aperture stop doubles/halves brightness, which reads as extreme on screen); 0.5-0.7 matches the tone response of a real camera's processed image. |
+| Neutral EV | -6 to 24 (slider 4-14) | 9.0 | EV100 that renders as a neutral image; the response compression pivots here. |
+| EV Compensation | -3 to 3 | 0 | EV bias, +1 = one stop brighter. In Manual mode this is a true stop. |
+
+### Depth Of Field
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Enable Depth of Field | bool | false | Compute and apply DOF blur from aperture + focus distance (game mode only). |
+| Focus Falloff | 1-10 (slider 1-6) | 3 | How far past the depth of field limits the blur keeps ramping. 1 = fully blurred right at the limits (the engine default, a very thin focus zone); higher values widen the sharp zone. |
+| Highlight Threshold | 0-20 (slider 0-8) | 2.0 | Scene HDR luminance a defocused pixel must exceed before it throws a bokeh sprite on top of the ordinary blur. Lower = more pixels splat. A soft knee of +/-50% around this value fades them in so they do not pop as the aperture moves. Only used when Use Sprite Bokeh is on. |
+| Highlight Splat Gain | 0-8 (slider 0-4) | 3.0 | Brightness of each bokeh sprite. A splat spreads a fixed amount of energy across its disc, so this scales how much is added back on top of the blur. Raise it if shaped bokeh reads too faint; the renderer already compensates for how much of the disc a shape texture actually covers. |
+
+### Film Grain
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Enable Film Grain | bool | true | Drive `FilterGrain_Amount` from ISO, aperture and shutter following a real sensor noise curve: clean at base ISO, climbing steeply past ISO 6400, boosted when the lens is starved of light. |
+| Grain Strength | 0-4 (slider 0-1) | 1.0 | Scales the computed grain before it reaches the renderer. |
+
+### Halation
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Halation | 0-3 (slider 0-2) | 0.0 | Red-orange halo around sources that overload the emulsion: light passes through the film, reflects off the base and re-exposes the red-sensitive layer around the source. A film-stock property, so it does not follow the f-number. 0 = off. |
+| Halation Threshold | 0-50 (slider 0-20) | 2.0 | Scene HDR value a source must exceed to halate. High on purpose: a bright sky must not halate, only sources that overload the emulsion. |
+| Halation Radius | 0-1 | 0.5 | How far the halo spreads from the source. |
+| Halation Tint | ColorF | (1.0, 0.35, 0.15) | Colour of the halo. Red-orange for colour negative stock (the red layer sits deepest); a neutral tint mimics a stock whose anti-halation backing is partly working. |
+
+### Motion Blur
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Enable Motion Blur | bool | true | Point the renderer's `r_MotionBlurShutterSpeed` at the camera shutter: 1/50 is the classic 180 degree film blur, fast shutters give crisp staccato motion. `r_MotionBlur` is set to 2 when it is 0. |
+
+### White Balance
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Enable White Balance | bool | true | Apply the camera's colour temperature on top of the scene grading. |
+| Color Temperature (K) | 1500-15000 (slider 2500-10000) | 6500 | White balance in Kelvin. 3200 tungsten, 5600 daylight, 6500 neutral. |
+
+The semantics are camera-referred: the gains make light of the SET temperature render white.
+Dialing 3200K in an (engine default) daylight scene reads **blue** — a tungsten setting used
+in daylight — while 8000K reads warm, exactly like a stills camera. The gains are luminance
+normalised so exposure is unaffected, and multiplied on top of the scene grading's colour
+balance rather than replacing it; external (TOD) writes are adopted as the new base so the
+per-frame multiply never compounds. 6500K is a no-op (the effect restores itself).
+
+### Diffraction Streaks
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Enable Diffraction Streaks | bool | true | Grow sunstars out of very bright sources the way a real iris diffracts light off its blade edges: invisible wide open, appearing around f/8, sharp by f/16. |
+| Blade Rotation (deg) | 0-360 | 0 | Phase of the iris, rotating the whole set of rays. Rays are spread evenly, so the pattern repeats every 360 / ray count degrees. This same rotation also turns the bokeh polygon — one iris, one orientation. |
+
+### Viewfinder
+
+Operator aids drawn over the final frame. They are **overlays**: never part of the picture,
+editor-live so framing can be judged while authoring, and trivially switchable off for
+recording.
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Frame Guides | bool | false | Draw the selected framing guides over the frame. Viewfinder overlay, never part of the picture. |
+| Rule Of Thirds | bool | true | Lines at 1/3 and 2/3 of both axes. |
+| Centre Cross | bool | true | Short cross at the frame centre, 6% of the frame height per arm. |
+| Safe Areas | bool | false | Action safe (centred 90% rectangle) and title safe (80%). |
+| Aspect Guide | bool | false | Outline the target aspect band and shade the outside. |
+| Guide Aspect | 1-4 | 2.39 | Target aspect of the framing guide (2.39, 1.85, 1.33 ...). |
+| Guide Opacity | 0-1 | 0.6 | Line opacity. The shading outside the aspect band is half of it. |
+| Focus Peaking | 0-2 | 0.0 | Tint in-focus edges. 0 = off. |
+| Peaking Colour | 1-5 | 1 | 1 red, 2 green, 3 blue, 4 yellow, 5 white. |
+| Overlays In Game Mode | bool | false | Keep the overlays on in game / simulation mode. Off = editor only, so a recording never contains them. |
+
+---
+
+## Viewfinder overlays
+
+**Frame guides** are monitor markings, so they are drawn in viewport space, not in picture
+space: they do not squeeze with an anamorphic presentation and they do not bend with lens
+distortion - a guide that moved with the image would stop telling the operator where the edges
+of the frame are. The aspect guide is different from the letterbox: the letterbox is part of
+the picture (a gate mask that is recorded), the aspect guide only outlines the band of the
+target aspect and shades what falls outside it, so a 2.39 composition can be judged inside a
+16:9 viewport without committing to the crop.
+
+**Focus peaking** tints edges that are inside the depth of field. The in-focus band is exactly
+this camera's thin-lens depth of field (`ComputeDOFBounds`, the same near/far pair the DOF
+model uses), with a 5% soft falloff on either limit, so a focus pull moves the tinted region
+with the focus - the near and far limits are re-pushed every frame. Edge response is the
+largest absolute luminance difference to the four neighbours of the final image, so what is
+tinted is what actually reads as sharp on screen, not a depth threshold.
+
+**Guide legend.** While the guides are on, the component prints two small white labels over
+the frame (same opacity as the lines): the aspect of the framing guide, just inside the top
+left corner of its band, and a framing readout in the bottom left -
+`50mm  f/2.8  focus 3.20m  Full Frame`. The stop switches to the T-stop
+(`T2.9`) as soon as the glass transmits less than all of the light, because that is the
+number a lens is actually pulled by. These are aux-text overlays and follow the same kill
+switch as the guides.
+
+**Diagnosing peaking.** `r_LensPeakingDebug 1` paints the in-focus band as a solid tint with
+the edge response dropped, so the band itself is visible; `r_LensPeakingDebug 2` paints the
+scene depth as a grey ramp (black at the camera, white at twice the far limit) with the band
+tinted on top. Both need `r_LensPeaking` above 0. If mode 1 shows a band in the right place
+but normal peaking shows nothing, the depth is fine and the picture simply has no edges
+there; if mode 2 is flat, the depth read is what is broken.
+
+**Recording safety.** Everything runs at the very end of the PostAA composite, after grain and
+sharpening, so the overlays are clean of both; nothing upstream of them is touched. They are
+driven by `r_LensOverlays`, which the component sets to 1 only while editing (or when
+**Overlays In Game Mode** is on) and to 0 otherwise, and which can be set to 0 by hand as a
+global kill switch. With the overlays off the composite is bit-for-bit the image it produced
+before this feature existed.
+
+---
+
+## Exposure modes
+
+Only active with `Enable Exposure Control` on. The component default is **Auto + Bias
+(game)**.
+
+### Manual (physical)
+
+The standard photographic equation gives EV100, and the tone mapper's `[EV Min, EV Max]`
+window is collapsed onto a single value — exposure comes entirely from the lens, which is the
+only way the f-number can change brightness:
+
+```
+EV100   = log2(N^2 / (transmission * t)) - log2(ISO / 100) - EVCompensation + NDstops
+applied = NeutralEV + (EV100 - NeutralEV) * ExposureResponse
+```
+
+`applied` is clamped to [-6, 24] and written to both EV Min and EV Max. Exposure Response 1
+restores strict physics; the default 0.6 mimics the S curve a camera's image processing rolls
+the extremes off with.
+
+### Auto + Bias (game) — AutoBiased
+
+Keeps the scene's own adaptation window and shifts it by the lens's **relative** stops:
+
+```
+bias           = (EV100 - NeutralEV) * ExposureResponse
+EV Min/EV Max  = scene value + bias (clamped -6..24); the auto compensation term is untouched
+```
+
+The engine's night is artist-lit and only a few stops darker than its day, so an absolute EV
+lock blows out night scenes; this mode lets auto exposure keep tracking the scene while the
+lens still visibly biases it. The base window tracks external (TOD) writes so the shift never
+compounds frame by frame.
+
+### Auto Compensation (legacy)
+
+Writes only `E3DPARAM_HDR_EYEADAPTATION_PARAMS.z`, the scene-key auto compensation strength.
+The engine scales that term by how far the scene is from the reference key, so its sign flips
+between bright and dark scenes and the aperture cannot affect brightness through it. Kept for
+compatibility with older setups.
+
+---
+
+## T-stop: radiometry on T, geometry on f
+
+`Transmission` defines the T-stop:
+
+```
+T = f / sqrt(transmission)
+```
+
+**Radiometry (exposure, the grain light term) follows T** — glass losses darken the image
+exactly like a real lens — while **geometry (DOF, bokeh size and shape, diffraction) stays on
+the f-number**. Whenever the lens changes, the console logs the effective T-stop
+(`[CinematicCamera] f/2.8 at transmission 0.81 -> T3.1`).
+
+---
+
+## Depth of field and bokeh
+
+### Thin lens
+
+```
+CoC limit c = sensorDiagonal(mm) / 1500 / 1000    (metres)
+near = d*f^2 / (f^2 + N*c*(d-f))
+far  = d*f^2 / (f^2 - N*c*(d-f))     (denominator <= 0 -> infinity)
+```
+
+### Focus Falloff
+
+The DOF shader has no sharp band; it computes
+
+```hlsl
+coc = saturate(|depth - focusDistance| / (focusRange * 0.5))^2 * blurAmount
+```
+
+so feeding it the physical depth of field width puts *full* blur exactly where the image
+should still be acceptably sharp. The component feeds `dofWidth * FocusFalloff` instead,
+which puts the physical DOF limits at `1/falloff^2` of maximum blur (11% at the default 3):
+the depth of field reads as sharp and the falloff beyond it stays smooth.
+
+### Blur amount
+
+```
+blurAmount = clamp(focalLength / max(N, 0.7) / 60, 0, 4)    // reference 85mm f/1.4 ~ 60mm
+```
+
+Deliberately not clamped at 1.0: long lenses wide open produce genuinely larger discs, but
+the gather kernel is still 49 taps, so watch for sparse/ringy discs at the top end.
+
+### Bokeh polygon
+
+The component writes `Dof_User_ApertureBlades`, `Dof_User_FNumber`, `Dof_User_BladeCurvature`
+and `Dof_User_BladeRotation` every frame. The renderer's kernel crosses from a circle to the
+blade polygon over an f/1.2 - f/2.8 window, but **only for curved blades**: at Blade Curvature
+0 the polygon is fully formed at every aperture (and sharpened further, on purpose), and at
+curvature 1 the behaviour reduces to the plain f-stop window. The visual strength of the
+polygon is exaggerated by `r_DepthOfFieldBokehShapeStrength` (default 2.0), because the
+physical radius modulation is far too subtle at high blade counts (8% for an octagon).
+`r_DepthOfFieldBokehQuality N` (N >= 3) forces an N-sided kernel for diagnostics. See
+[docs/ConsoleReference.md](docs/ConsoleReference.md).
+
+### Sprite bokeh (hybrid highlights)
+
+**Use Sprite Bokeh** switches `r_DepthOfFieldMode` to 0, the hybrid path: the full gather blur
+runs exactly as in mode 1, and a splat layer is added on top for out-of-focus **highlights**
+only. A pixel splats when its scene HDR luminance exceeds **Highlight Threshold** (soft knee
+of +/-50% around it), and each splat deposits energy scaled by **Highlight Splat Gain**. A
+scene with no bright highlights therefore looks the same in either mode. The cvar is saved and
+restored like the motion blur cvars, so leaving the camera never strands the renderer in a
+mode nobody asked for.
+
+### Shape mask and Mask Aperture
+
+**Bokeh Shape Texture** is the silhouette (red channel) of a physical mask mounted in front of
+the lens, pushed to the `Dof_User_BokehShapeTex` post param. It only does anything while the
+hybrid splats are running, and it is rewritten every frame on purpose: the param texture keeps
+one slot per render command buffer, and a name written once reaches only one of them.
+
+Two separate aperture mechanisms - the mask's value field and the blade polygon - combined as a
+**physical intersection**: the pupil is `mask ∩ iris` at every f-number, with
+`k = Mask Aperture / f-number` the iris radius in mask units.
+
+- **k > 1** (iris opened wider than the mask): the mask bounds the pupil; the polygon only trims
+  whatever of the silhouette pokes past it (the outermost corners first). The gather taps are
+  importance-sampled from the same clipped pupil, so even the soft halo takes the silhouette.
+- **k < 1** (iris stopped down past the mask): the polygon bounds the pupil and the mask is
+  sampled at that scale, so the silhouette is clipped corner by corner and rounded off
+  progressively until the iris sits inside the cut-out's open centre and the plain polygon
+  remains. The fill style (radial iris look vs mask value field) follows whichever hole is the
+  smaller one.
+
+The pupil is rebuilt on the CPU in quantized iris steps (the same 64x64 mask pixels the gather
+kernel samples), and its fill fraction of the splat quad drives the energy gain exactly - a
+thin cut-out deposits as much light as the disc it replaces at any f-number.
+
+The mask is an optical element, not just a shape. The thin-lens depth of field and the maximum
+blur follow the pupil's bounding circle, `max(f-number, Mask Aperture)`
+(`GetGeometricAperture()`), so a note cut-out at f/2 in front of an f/1.2 iris produces
+f/2-sized discs; and the light reaching the sensor is `area(iris ∩ mask) / area(iris)`,
+integrated from the mask texture for the current f-number, times **Mask Open Area** (tint),
+through the T-stop transmission term - so the exposure modes and the film grain light term
+react to the partly blocked hole exactly like a neutral density loss, continuously. The
+renderer still receives the raw iris f-number. The T-stop log line reports the effective
+numbers: `f/1.2 with mask f/2.0 (iris/mask radius 1.67, tint 100%) -> effective transmission
+0.21, T2.6, CoC from f/2.0`.
+
+The renderer compensates a textured splat for how little of its bounding square a mask passes
+(~0.35 for the engine's own bokeh masks), so a star reads as present as the circle it
+replaces. Domain and shape-texture transitions are logged once per change (`[DOF] gather
+domain=...`, `[DOF] shape texture '...' -> resolved ...`).
+
+---
+
+## Diffraction streak physics
+
+Light bends around the straight edge of every iris blade, and each edge throws a pair of rays:
+
+- **Even blade count**: opposing rays overlap -> **N rays**. **Odd count**: offset -> **2N**.
+  The ray count is clamped to [4, 24] (the renderer draws them as 12 symmetric line passes at
+  most, each painting two opposed spikes).
+- Brightness: `amount = ((N - 6.3) / (11 - 6.3))^1.2`, clamped 0-1 — nothing below f/6.3,
+  **saturated by f/11**, after which stopping down reads as the rays growing rather than
+  brightening.
+- Length: `length = (N - 7) / (22 - 7)`, clamped 0-1 — the **length curve owns f/7 to f/22**.
+- `Blade Rotation` goes straight into `HDR_Streaks_Angle` (and into the bokeh polygon's
+  rotation).
+
+These are the `HDR_Streaks_*` post effect params; an unmodified renderer does not know them
+(reads return 0, writes are silently dropped), so the effect needs this renderer/shader set.
+Master cvar: `r_HDRStreaks` (2 = diagnostic force mode).
+
+---
+
+## Film grain model
+
+```
+sensorStops = log2(36 / sensorWidth)                    // full frame +0, APS-C ~ +0.6, M4/3 ~ +1.1
+g    = clamp((log2(ISO/100) + sensorStops) / 8, 0, 1)   // 8 stops, ISO 100-25600
+base = 0.25*g^1.2 + g^3.5                               // visible from ISO 400, top stops get dirty fast
+lightStops = log2((transmission*t / N^2) / ((1/50) / 5.6^2)) - NDstops
+boost = clamp(2^(-lightStops * 0.35), 0.6, 2.2)         // starved sensor noisier, flooded cleaner
+grain = clamp(base * boost, 0, 1.5) * GrainStrength
+```
+
+The reference exposure is f/5.6 at 1/50 s. The result goes to `FilterGrain_Amount`, which
+PostAA combines with the TOD environment grain by `max()`, so the per-frame write cannot be
+stomped.
+
+---
+
+## Focal stepping and servo
+
+The engine takes a continuous FOV, so detents are produced by quantising the focal length that
+feeds the optics; the stored property still holds whatever the fader sent.
+
+| Mode | Behaviour |
+| --- | --- |
+| Continuous | No quantisation. |
+| Prime Lens Set (default) | Snap in log space to the classic prime series: 8/10/12/14/16/18/20/24/28/35/40/50/65/75/85/100/135/150/180/200/250/300/400/500/600 mm. |
+| Geometric | `Steps Per Doubling` equal ratio detents per doubling (default 6, about x1.12 per step). |
+
+**Focal Step Time** turns the jump into a broadcast servo zoom rather than a hard cut: the
+quantised target is approached in log space with a framerate independent exponential ease that
+settles within the given time.
+
+```
+alpha = 1 - exp(-dt * 3 / FocalStepTime)
+f <- 2^( log2(f) + (log2(target) - log2(f)) * alpha )
+```
+
+Continuous mode, Focal Step Time 0 and the editor all snap directly.
+
+---
+
+## Focus breathing
+
+A unit-focus lens extends to focus close, so the effective focal length grows with
+magnification and the view tightens:
+
+```
+m     = f / (d - f)                  // d clamped to >= 2f
+f_eff = f * (1 + BreathingIntensity * m)
+```
+
+FOV only; 0 = an internally corrected cine lens that does not breathe.
+
+---
+
+## Lens character: vignette and distortion
+
+Both are applied through cvars (`r_HDRVignetteAmount`, `r_LensDistortion`), overwritten every
+frame while the camera drives the render state (in the editor too) and restored when it stops;
+the console can still audition values when no cinematic camera is active.
+
+```
+wideOpenness = clamp((5.6 - N) / (5.6 - 1.4), 0, 1)
+vignette     = VignetteAmount * (1 + VignetteApertureLink * (wideOpenness - 1) * 0.85)
+
+focalTerm  = log2(35 / f_eff)
+distortion = clamp(Distortion + DistortionFocalLink * focalTerm * 0.5, -1, 1) * 0.35
+```
+
+---
+
+## Anamorphic
+
+An anamorphic lens carries a cylindrical element that compresses a wider horizontal field onto
+the same sensor. One property, `Squeeze Factor` (S), drives three physically distinct
+consequences, each on its own path through the renderer.
+
+**1. Field of view and the gate mask.** The projected (desqueezed) format is
+`sensorWidth * S` by `sensorHeight`, so its aspect is `formatAspect = sensorWidth * S / sensorHeight`
+(36 x 24 mm at S = 2 gives 3.0:1). When that format is wider than the viewport, the format's
+*width* is what has to fit: the horizontal field of view comes from the widened capture and the
+vertical field is derived through the viewport aspect, with black bars taking the rest.
+
+```
+hFOV = 2 atan(sensorWidth * S / 2f)
+vFOV = 2 atan(tan(hFOV / 2) / viewportAspect)      // formatAspect > viewportAspect
+vFOV = 2 atan(sensorHeight / 2f)                   // otherwise (spherical, or the format fits)
+```
+
+The bars are drawn in the final composite from the `r_LensLetterbox` cvar, which the component
+pushes (and restores) exactly like `r_LensDistortion`: `formatAspect` when the mask is needed,
+0 otherwise. They are hard-edged, as a real gate mask is. At S = 1 nothing changes.
+
+**Show Squeezed Frame** swaps the presentation for the raw anamorphic negative: the same band
+the gate mask would keep is stretched vertically to fill the viewport (`r_LensLetterbox` goes
+negative), so the whole picture carries the squeeze - circles become tall ovals, faces go thin -
+not just the bokeh. The camera frustum is identical in both presentations; switching between
+them changes nothing about what is in frame, only how the band is shown.
+
+**2. Oval bokeh.** The pupil image is compressed horizontally by the same factor, so an
+out-of-focus point spreads into a vertical oval of `height : width = S : 1`. The gather kernel
+divides every finished tap's horizontal component by S (both the polygon and the shape-mask
+kernels go through this), and the sprite bokeh splat narrows its quad the same way, dividing
+the energy-conserving deposit by the oval's real area so brightness is unchanged.
+
+**3. Horizontal flare.** The cylindrical front element throws one long tinted line across the
+frame instead of a star. It is a separate effect on its own switch (`Horizontal Flare`), with
+its own threshold and its own single horizontal march of three widening iterations - eight taps
+each, so `8^3` tap distances span the whole frame width. It follows the *opposite* f-number
+curve to the iris diffraction: strongest with the whole element in the light path and gone by
+f/11.
+
+```
+wideOpenness = clamp((11 - N) / (11 - 2), 0, 1)     // 1 at f/2 and wider, 0 at f/11
+amount       = HorizontalFlare * wideOpenness
+```
+
+The flare is pushed whenever the camera drives the render state, regardless of
+`Enable Diffraction Streaks` - one is a property of the iris, the other of the front element.
+Both ride the `HDR_Streaks_*` post effect params into the bloom target.
+
+---
+
+## Halation
+
+On film, light from a very bright source does not stop at the emulsion. It passes through,
+reflects off the film base and re-exposes the emulsion from behind, in a halo far wider than
+the source itself. In a colour negative the red-sensitive layer sits deepest, so what comes
+back is red-orange, its edge is soft, and it only appears around sources that genuinely
+**overload** the emulsion - never around a merely bright sky.
+
+Halation is a property of the **film stock**, not of the lens. Unlike the diffraction streaks
+and the anamorphic flare, nothing here is modulated by the f-number: the four properties are
+pushed to the renderer exactly as authored, every frame, the same way the streak params are.
+
+The pass chain lives in `CBloomStage::ExecuteHalation()`, in the **bloom domain** - it runs
+right after the streaks, reuses the two quarter-resolution HDR scratch targets they leave
+free, and blends into `$HDRFinalBloom` with the same bloom-weight compensation, so it costs no
+permanent memory and needs no second tone-map hook:
+
+1. **Threshold** the half-res HDR scene at `Halation Threshold` with a soft knee of a quarter
+   of the threshold (the `HDRStreakThreshold` technique).
+2. **Spread** it wide, horizontally then vertically, through `HDRStreakStretch` with an
+   isotropic direction, a stride of `2 + 10 * radius` quarter-res texels and a near-flat per
+   tap decay of 0.90. This is what sets the halo's width: at radius 0.5 the 7 taps per side
+   reach 49 texels, about 196 full-res pixels on a 1080p frame.
+3. **Round** the profile with two `CGaussianBlurPass` passes at distribution `3 + 5 * radius`.
+   That utility's 16 bilinear taps are pinned to +-8 texels whatever the distribution, so it
+   cannot widen anything - it only smears the discrete tap gaps into a continuous soft edge.
+4. **Composite** into the bloom target with the stretch shader at a **zero** step (every tap
+   lands on the same texel, so the normalised sum is a plain tinted sample), tinted by
+   `Halation Tint` and scaled by `amount * 8 / bloomCompositeWeight`. The x8 gain is there
+   because an energy-conserving blur spreads a source over thousands of texels: without it the
+   halo is physically right and visually invisible.
+
+These are the `HDR_Halation_*` post effect params; an unmodified renderer does not know them
+(reads return 0, writes are silently dropped), so the effect needs this renderer/shader set.
+Master cvar: `r_HDRHalation` (**2 = diagnostic force mode**: amount 1, threshold 0.5, radius
+0.7, independent of the game-side params).
+
+---
+
+## Build and deployment
+
+Both plugins hang off the engine's `Code/CryPlugins/CMakeLists.txt`. The bundled CMake is
+mandatory (a system CMake 4.x fails on `CMP0040 OLD` in `InitialSetup.cmake`):
+
+```
+Tools\CMake\Win32\bin\cmake.exe -S . -B solutions_cmake\win64
+Tools\CMake\Win32\bin\cmake.exe --build solutions_cmake\win64 --config Profile --target CinematicCamera
+```
+
+Deployment:
+
+1. Copy `bin\win_x64\CinematicCamera.dll` to the engine directory actually being run
+   (`cryengine-57-lts\5.7.1\bin\win_x64\`) and to the gamesdk-sample-project2 project's `bin\`
+   directory.
+2. Deploy the modified shaders (polygonal/sprite bokeh DOF, streaks) as loose files to the
+   engine's `engine\shaders\HWScripts\CryFX\`.
+
+Add to the `.cryproject` plugin list:
+
+```json
+{ "guid": "", "type": "EType::Native", "path": "CinematicCamera" },
+```
